/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:1565: UndefinedMetricWarning: Precision is ill-defined and being set to 0.0 in labels with no predicted samples. Use `zero_division` parameter to control this behavior.\n  _warn_prf(average, modifier, f\"{metric.capitalize()} is\", len(result))\n/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:1565: UndefinedMetricWarning: Precision is ill-defined and being set to 0.0 in labels with no predicted samples. Use `zero_division` parameter to control this behavior.\n  _warn_prf(average, modifier, f\"{metric.capitalize()} is\", len(result))\n/usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:1565: UndefinedMetricWarning: Precision is ill-defined and being set to 0.0 in labels with no predicted samples. Use `zero_division` parameter to control this behavior.\n  _warn_prf(average, modifier, f\"{metric.capitalize()} is\", len(result))\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":105},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<h2>Classification Metrics Explained</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Precision</h3>\n<p>\nPrecision measures how many of the <b>predicted positive</b> samples were actually positive.<br>\nIt answers: <i>\"When the model predicted positive, how often was it correct?\"</i>\n</p>\n<p><b>Formula:</b> Precision = TP / (TP + FP)</p>\n\n<h3>2. Recall</h3>\n<p>\nRecall measures how many of the <b>actual positive</b> samples were correctly identified.<br>\nIt answers: <i>\"Out of all real positives, how many did the model detect?\"</i>\n</p>\n<p><b>Formula:</b> Recall = TP / (TP + FN)</p>\n\n<h3>3. F1-Score</h3>\n<p>\nF1-score is the <b>harmonic mean</b> of Precision and Recall.<br>\nIt balances Precision & Recall, especially useful for <u>imbalanced datasets</u>.\n</p>\n<p><b>Formula:</b> F1 = 2 × (Precision × Recall) / (Precision + Recall)</p>\n\n<h3>4. Support</h3>\n<p>\nSupport is the number of actual samples of each class present in the dataset.<br>\nIt shows how many true instances of the class were there during evaluation.\n</p>\n<p><b>Example:</b> If class \"Attack\" appears 500 times in the test set, its support = 500.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>Summary Table</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\">\n<tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Focus</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Precision</td><td>How many predicted positives are correct</td><td>False Positives</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recall</td><td>How many actual positives were correctly detected</td><td>False Negatives</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F1-Score</td><td>Balanced harmonic mean of Precision & Recall</td><td>Both FP & FN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Support</td><td>Number of true samples per class</td><td>Class distribution</td></tr>\n</table>\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<h3>Macro Average</h3>\n<p>\nMacro average computes the metric (precision, recall, or F1-score) for <b>each class independently</b>, \nand then takes the <b>simple arithmetic mean</b> of those scores. <br><br>\nIt treats <b>all classes equally</b> regardless of their support (class frequency).\n</p>\n<p><b>Key idea:</b> Every class has equal weight.</p>\n<p><b>Formula:</b> (Score_class1 + Score_class2 + ... + Score_classN) / N</p>\n<p><b>Best use case:</b> When you want to evaluate performance on <u>minority classes</u> or when classes have <u>imbalanced support</u>.</p>\n\n<hr>\n\n<h3>Weighted Average</h3>\n<p>\nWeighted average computes the metric (precision, recall, or F1-score) for <b>each class independently</b>, \nbut then averages them using the <b>support (number of true instances)</b> of each class as weights. <br><br>\nThis means <b>majority classes influence the score more</b> than minority classes.\n</p>\n<p><b>Key idea:</b> Classes contribute based on their support.</p>\n<p><b>Formula:</b> (Score_class1 × support1 + Score_class2 × support2 + ... + Score_classN × supportN) / total_samples</p>\n<p><b>Best use case:</b> When you want an overall score that reflects the <u>distribution of the dataset</u>.</p>\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df.shape","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-28T07:27:01.852783Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-28T07:27:01.853027Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-28T07:27:01.85916Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-28T07:27:01.853009Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-28T07:27:01.858372Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":73,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"(125973, 58)"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":73},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"<h1>here i am training agin for my wesite</h1>","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"x_train = df.drop(columns='n_label')\ny_train = df['n_label']","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-28T07:27:03.869054Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-28T07:27:03.869348Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-28T07:27:03.884719Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-28T07:27:03.869323Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-28T07:27:03.883363Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":74},{"cell_type":"code","source":"x_train.shape","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.315995Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.316301Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.322151Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.316275Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.3212Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":75,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"(125973, 57)"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":75},{"cell_type":"code","source":"y_train.shape","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.756699Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.757Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.76294Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.756974Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-28T07:27:04.761968Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":76,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"(125973,)"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_